The propuse of calibrtion is to make sure that the robot function properly and move properly. 
IMU calibration:This establishes the robot's fundamental orientation relative to gravity by zeroing the internal accelerometers and gyroscopes. It ensures the system correctly identifies a perfectly level plane. If the IMU is miscalibrated, the G1 will attempt to compensate for a non-existent slope, resulting in constant drifting, leaning, or an inability to stand still on flat ground.

Full-Body Calibration (Joint Zero-Point Calibration): This registers the absolute mechanical "zero" position for every joint encoder across the entire robot simultaneously. During this process, motor torque is cut, and the operator manually rotates the waist, legs, and arms counterclockwise until they hit their physical hard stops. Saving this state tells the kinematic solver the exact mechanical boundaries. If this is off, the robot's calculated posture will desync from its physical posture, causing swaying, foot-dragging, or irregular walking rhythms.



